research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:99:### A5 — Amplitudes, dispersion relations, positivity, and bootstrap (`aqft-eft-amplitudes`)
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:101:Define the external stable states, LSZ/scattering data if such a construction is available, Mandelstam variables, crossing action, partial-wave normalization, amplitude analytic domain, poles/cuts, IR regulator and subtraction point. Start with a massive, gapped scalar elastic channel with a finite forward limit and an explicitly stated real-analytic, crossing-symmetric, unitary amplitude and polynomial high-energy bound. Derive one forward dispersion inequality in that narrow setting. Only after this base result may the scaffold add spinning/multifield channels, nonforward bounds, massless gauge particles or gravity.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:103:Candidate items: `def-aqft-scattering-amplitude-input`; `lem-aqft-crossing-even-forward-amplitude`; `thm-aqft-gapped-scalar-forward-positivity`; `def-aqft-truncated-amplitude-bootstrap-problem`; `prop-aqft-finite-truncated-bootstrap-constraints`; and `ex-aqft-scalar-positivity-bound`. The B page should show both an allowed and a forbidden Wilson-coefficient region under exactly the same assumptions. `thm-qftx-rational-bcfw-residue` is reusable only for its conditional rational tree recursion. The new positivity proof still owes the contour argument, crossing/symmetrization, pole subtraction, subtractions at infinity, optical-theorem spectral sign, and low-energy coefficient identification.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:145:| J. Tokuda, K. Aoki, S. Hirano, “Gravitational Positivity Bounds,” arXiv:2007.15009v2 | Abstract and sections 2–3 inventory inspected: graviton `t`-channel pole, Regge assumption, finite correction and possible controlled positivity violation. | No universal gravitational positivity theorem is claimed; exact bounds depend on Regge and IR assumptions. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:146:| L. Buoninfante, L.-Q. Shao, A. Tokareva, “Non-local positivity bounds: islands in Terra Incognita,” arXiv:2412.08634v2 (19 Feb 2025) | Abstract and introduction, pp. 1–2: polynomial boundedness is an explicit locality-related assumption; exponential boundedness yields different bounds. | Source is a research paper on a broadened UV class, not a consensus replacement for local-QFT bounds. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-eft-experiments.md:159:The useful minimal mathematical sequence is: (1) formal series, finite-dimensional linear algebra, polynomial modules, Lie/reductive group actions and invariant quotients; (2) test-function/distribution and Fourier tools for local actions and regulated perturbative amplitudes; (3) finite-regulator Gaussian integration for the one existing exact shell model; (4) ODE existence and matrix evolution for explicitly truncated RG systems; (5) complex analysis/residue/dispersion integrals and a spectral/optical-theorem bridge for amplitude positivity; and (6) measurable probability, likelihoods, profile-likelihood ratio and coverage methods for reported inference. Missing bridges must be added as mathematical-domain items with mathematical-only dependencies or treated as open obligations. Physics postulates, model interpretations, detector calibration assumptions, and measured input remain physical/empirical interfaces.
