../items/lem-regular-unit-disks-have-nonlinear-localized-incidence-responses-with-moving-slits.md:46:[F3] The actual Euclidean inverse and higher inverse theorems and circle Cauchy formula give local inverses, holomorphic derivatives and coefficient bounds on fixed separated neighborhoods. ([[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]], [[thm-higher-regularity-of-local-inverses]], [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]])
../items/lem-regular-unit-disks-have-nonlinear-localized-incidence-responses-with-moving-slits.md:58:5.1 We justify the global incidence and slit assertions. Off the forced root patch the coefficients are holomorphic and close in every fixed higher norm to the original ones. Near each interior cover branch the original incidence curve has equation $z-p_0(w)=0$ times a holomorphic unit with nonzero $z$ derivative. The nearby coefficient curve consequently has a unique graph $z=p_h(w)$ there by the actual implicit inverse, so its root map is still a local biholomorphism. On the forced patch it is the graph of the orientation-positive inverse from step3.1. On the remaining simple-root patches the same statement follows by continuity. The inactive roots solve the ordinary holomorphic equation with fixed real boundary tangent circle and fixed node value; as actual root maps their images stay in one small holomorphic circle chart, so the imaginary-part gradient identity makes them the original constants. Near the physical boundary all roots are distinct and holomorphic, with real analytic circle values. The derivative of a labelled root has exactly the original finitely many simple zeros in the reflected tip disks: the holomorphic local inverse theorem for its derivative, and a lower bound on the remaining compact part of each disk, give one simple zero there; reflection makes that zero real. Equivalently the circle coefficient bound and its local power factor exclude any additional zero. On the compact boundary remainder the derivative stays nonzero. Thus no new INTERIOR root critical point is hidden near a tip. Thus each old proper slit has its two nearby simple tips and the same regular marked bank. The finite point-incidence degrees are unchanged by the small boundary-preserving map homotopy. All active local root maps are orientation preserving, including on the forced patch. Their actual topological degree over every open cut region is therefore still zero or one. Two different interior preimages, or an interior and boundary-bank preimage, would duplicate an open off-circle region and contradict this degree identity, exactly as in the unit-incidence proof. Hence the active root remains globally one-to-one in the interior; the bank/tip classification gives the same labelled slit image. Its near-tip coordinates have $w=a_h+\zeta_h^2$, obtained by factoring the simple derivative zero and taking the local square root of its nonzero holomorphic unit. Their coefficients depend smoothly on $h$ by step4.1 and the fixed-contour Cauchy formula. Away from tips use the target root charts; near tips use these actual source coordinates. They give a finite smooth family of incidence and boundary charts. All marked root coordinates are unchanged by the physical node constraint, and they stay off the tips. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, construct, algebra]
../items/lem-local-hartogs-extension-across-polydisc-shells.md:48:[L2] Cauchy's integral formula on a circle recovers a holomorphic one-variable function from any smaller concentric circle ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]).
../items/lem-the-weighted-discrete-series-space-is-a-hilbert-space.md:67:[F3] A function holomorphic on a disc satisfies the Cauchy integral formula on every circle compactly contained in it ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]), equals its Taylor series throughout the largest centred disc in its domain ([[thm-taylor-expansion-holomorphic-function]]), and its Taylor coefficients obey the Cauchy estimates $|c_n|\le M/r^n$ ([[cor-cauchy-estimates-taylor-coefficients]]); the coefficient bounds make the series converge absolutely and uniformly on every closed subdisc.
../items/lem-the-weighted-discrete-series-space-is-a-hilbert-space.md:87:1.1 Fix $z_0\in\mathfrak H$ and choose $r>0$ with $\overline{D(z_0,r)}\subset\mathfrak H$. The Cauchy formula and Cauchy–Schwarz on each circle centered at $z_0$ give $|f(z_0)|^2\le(2\pi)^{-1}\int_0^{2\pi}|f(z_0+se^{it})|^2dt$ for $0<s<r$; integrating with $2s/r^2$ yields $|f(z_0)|^2\le(\pi r^2)^{-1}\int_{D(z_0,r)}|f|^2dA$. Since $y^{n-2}$ has a positive lower bound on this disk, $|f(z_0)|\le C_{z_0,r}\|f\|_n$. If $z\in D(z_i,r_i/2)$, then $D(z,r_i/2)\subset D(z_i,r_i)$; applying the same estimate there and using a lower bound for the weight on $D(z_i,r_i)$ gives a uniform evaluation bound on $D(z_i,r_i/2)$. A finite subcover of any compact $C\subset\mathfrak H$ therefore gives one constant $C_C$ for all $z\in C$. [F3, A1, algebra]
../items/cor-cauchy-inequalities.md:44:[L1] The higher-derivative Cauchy formula gives $f^{(n)}(a)=n!(2\pi i)^{-1}\int_\gamma f(\zeta)/(\zeta-a)^{n+1}\,d\zeta$ on the positively oriented radius-$r$ circle ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-higher-derivatives]]).
