../items/thm-hartogs-figure-extension.md:46:[L3] If $|w|<s$, then the slice $z\mapsto f(z,w)$ is holomorphic on the whole unit disc, so the one-variable Cauchy formula recovers it from the circle $|\zeta|=\rho$ whenever $|z|<\rho<1$ ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]).
../items/thm-riemann-extension-across-hypersurface-zero-sets.md:45:[L3] A contour integral is holomorphic in one complex parameter, the polydisc Cauchy formula specializes to the usual one-variable formula, and a locally bounded separately holomorphic function is holomorphic ([[thm-contour-parameter-integrals-are-holomorphic]], [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-on-a-polydisc]], [[thm-locally-bounded-separate-holomorphy]]).
../items/thm-riemann-extension-across-hypersurface-zero-sets.md:61:3.1 For fixed $z'$, the one-variable Cauchy formula from [L3] applied to the holomorphic slice extension $F_{z'}$ from step 2.1 shows that the integral in step 2.2 equals $F_{z'}(z_m)$ for every $|z_m|<r$. In particular, when $W(z',z_m)\ne0$ this value is the original $f(z',z_m)$. So step 2.2 gives a local holomorphic extension in the chosen coordinates, and undoing the coordinate change extends $f$ across the original point $a$. By step 1.1 these local extensions agree on overlaps, and therefore glue to a unique global holomorphic extension on $U$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, L3] ∎
../items/lem-round-planar-real-boundary-pole-problem-has-a-bounded-normalized-response.md:44:[F3] The circle Cauchy formula controls derivatives of a holomorphic function on a smaller interior disk by its values on a containing circle; its circle and radial Cauchy--Schwarz versions give the corresponding interior $L^2$ estimates. ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]])
