../items/thm-holomorphic-spectral-mapping.md:55:[L5] Cauchy formula on a cycle: for holomorphic $h$ on open $\Omega$ and a cycle $\Gamma$ with trace in $\Omega$ null-homologous in $\Omega$, $n(\Gamma,p)h(p) = \frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_\Gamma h(\zeta)/(\zeta-p)d\zeta$ for $p \in \Omega\setminus\Gamma^\ast$, and the double integral of a continuous integrand over two such cycles may be iterated in either order ([[thm-global-cauchy-integral-formula-homology]], [[lem-resolvent-identity]], the mesh estimate of [[def-banach-algebra-valued-contour-integral]]).
../items/thm-holomorphic-spectral-mapping.md:73:5.2 Choice of the outer cycle and Cauchy evaluation: apply [L4] to the compact set $f[K]$ inside $V$, obtaining a cycle $\gamma$ with index $1$ on $f[K]$ and $0$ outside $V$; then for every $w \in \beta^\ast$ (so $f(w) \in f[K]$) the Cauchy formula [L5] applied to $g$ on $V$ along $\gamma$ gives $\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_\gamma\frac{g(z)}{z-f(w)}\,dz = n(\gamma,f(w))g(f(w)) = g(f(w))$. [step 4.1, L5, algebra]
../items/ex-spectral-projection-of-an-isolated-eigenvalue-agrees-with-the-riesz-projection.md:64:1.2 The scalar Cauchy kernel of the contour is the indicator of the enclosed disc: for every $\zeta\in\mathbb C\setminus\gamma([0,2\pi])$ one has $c(\zeta):=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint_\gamma(z-\zeta)^{-1}dz=1$ if $|\zeta-\lambda|<r$ and $c(\zeta)=0$ if $|\zeta-\lambda|>r$, by the Cauchy integral formula applied to $f\equiv1$ in the first case and Cauchy's theorem on the convex disc $D(\lambda,|\zeta-\lambda|)$ in the second. [A4]
../items/lem-winding-number-is-locally-constant-via-integral-estimate.md:25:    - title: "L. V. Ahlfors, Complex Analysis, 3rd ed., Ch. 4 §2.1-2.3 (the index of a closed curve, its local constancy, and the Cauchy integral formula)"
../items/thm-integrable-flat-grid-rectangle-counts-equal-the-full-filtered-grid-differential.md:63:1.1 First record the actual incidence construction needed for integrable strips, rather than import a clean-cover hypothesis. The two compact product tori avoid the diagonal, and the generator ends do too. Thus an actual strip with uniform generator ends meets the diagonal only in a compact interior part. Holomorphic local discriminants make this intersection discrete unless the whole map lies in the diagonal; the latter is excluded by its boundary. It is therefore finite. Off those points order its distinct roots locally. Their continuations give the finite degree-$n$ root-label cover of the punctured base, with monodromy permutations of the $n$ labels. At each omitted point separate its permutation into cycles of lengths $r$. In a cycle use the positive source coordinate $b-b_0=\zeta^r$; each root is now a single bounded holomorphic function of $\zeta$, and extends across zero. Bounded holomorphic removal follows directly from its Cauchy coefficients: every negative Laurent coefficient has absolute value at most $M\rho^{|k|}$ and vanishes as $\rho\to0$. This is the local construction in [F3], applied here to the actual strip. Near a physical boundary the roots are distinct and lie on their labelled straight row or column, so they extend by ordinary real-line Schwarz reflection; reflection follows by conjugating the upper and lower holomorphic pieces, whose matching real traces cancel the interface term, and the Cauchy integral formula. At the two ends, each distinct root has the exact transverse Fourier expansion in [F4]; in the quadrant coordinate $q=e^{\pi(s+it)/2}$, or $q=e^{-\pi(s+it)/2}$ at the other end, it is a convergent series of odd nonnegative powers with its generator constant. Hence it extends holomorphically at that corner. We obtain an actual compact bordered incidence surface with corners, a positive degree-$n$ branched base projection and a holomorphic root map to the torus. It has $n$ distinct root corners at each end, and only finite interior branching. No surface representativity assertion for arbitrary nonholomorphic classes was used. [F3, F4, given, construct, algebra]
../items/thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem.md:56:[L7] The polydisc Cauchy formula specializes to the usual one-variable Cauchy formula when only the last variable is present ([[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-on-a-polydisc]]).
../items/thm-weierstrass-preparation-theorem.md:66:3.1 Define $$u(z',z_m):=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{|\zeta|=r} \frac{f(z',\zeta)}{W(z',\zeta)(\zeta-z_m)}\,d\zeta.$$ Because $W(z',\zeta)\ne0$ on $|\zeta|=r$, the integrand is continuous on $V\times\{|\zeta|=r\}\times\{|z_m|<r\}$ and holomorphic in each parameter variable. By [L6], the resulting function $u$ is separately holomorphic and locally bounded, hence holomorphic on $V\times\{|z_m|<r\}$. For fixed $z'$, the one-variable Cauchy formula [L7] applied to the holomorphic slice quotient $q_{z'}$ from step 2.1 gives $$u(z',z_m)=q_{z'}(z_m),$$ so $$f(z',z_m)=u(z',z_m)W(z',z_m).$$ [step 2.1, L6, L7]
