-The index bound ensures every moment used has index at most $N$. Since $0<x(\nu)\le1$, both integrands are nonnegative. Each is bounded by $Cw(\nu)$ for $C=\sup_{x\in[0,1]}p(x)^2<\infty$, so [F6] and [F3] give convergent nonnegative improper integrals, using [F5] on every finite truncation. Both remaining families therefore hold. At threshold $x=1$ the second localizer vanishes, and as $\nu\to\infty$ one has $x\to0$; neither endpoint changes the nonnegative-integral argument. [F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
+3.1 Fix $r\ge0$ with $2r+1\le N$ and the same arbitrary vector, and use $p(x)=\sum_{i=0}^r a_i x^i$. The moment identities and [F4] give $\sum_{i,j=0}^r a_i a_j b_{i+j+1}=\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}w(\nu)x(\nu)p(x(\nu))^2\,d\nu$ and $\sum_{i,j=0}^r a_i a_j(b_{i+j}-b_{i+j+1})=\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}w(\nu)(1-x(\nu))p(x(\nu))^2\,d\nu$. The index bound ensures every moment used has index at most $N$. Since $0<x(\nu)\le1$, both integrands are nonnegative. Each is bounded by $Cw(\nu)$ for $C=\sup_{x\in[0,1]}p(x)^2<\infty$, so [F6] and [F3] give convergent nonnegative improper integrals, using [F5] on every finite truncation. Both remaining families therefore hold. At threshold $x=1$ the second localizer vanishes, and as $\nu\to\infty$ one has $x\to0$; neither endpoint changes the nonnegative-integral argument. [F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]
 
 4.1 For $N=1$, all three applicable families have $r=0$ and are included in steps 2.1 and 3.1. For every $N\ge1$, those steps establish every inequality in the defining index ranges, so the vector belongs to $\mathcal K_N$. Equality may occur when an integrand vanishes; the proof gives no strict inequality. The set $\mathcal K_N$ was defined only by these necessary tests, so this membership proves no converse or full-amplitude realization. [F1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
