+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let $m>0$, $\nu_0=2m^2$, and let $A(s)$, its real crossing-symmetric subtraction $P(s)$ of the specified isolated real subthreshold simple poles and their crossing partners, and $B(v)=A(2m^2+v)-P(2m^2+v)$ satisfy the gapped identical-scalar amplitude conditions defined above. In particular, all such variable poles are removed; $B$ is holomorphic on $\mathbb C\setminus(( -\infty,-\nu_0]\cup[\nu_0,\infty))$, real analytic, and crossing even; $B(v)=o(|v|^2)$ uniformly on the closing arcs; and no massless pole or cut reaches $t=0$. Its forward absorptive density $\rho(\nu)=\operatorname{Im}B(\nu+i0)$ is nonnegative, locally Riemann integrable with the cut-boundary limits specified above, and has finite $\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-3}\,d\nu$. Then for every integer $n\ge1$,
+
+$$\frac{B^{(2n)}(0)}{(2n)!}=\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\frac{\rho(\nu)}{\nu^{2n+1}}\,d\nu\ge0.$$
+
+In particular, the first nonconstant even Taylor coefficient $B''(0)/2!$ is nonnegative. Equality is allowed; no strict bound or sign for an arbitrary off-shell operator coefficient is asserted.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[def-aqft-scattering-amplitude-input]]
+
+[[lem-aqft-crossing-even-forward-amplitude]]
+
+[[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]
+
+[[thm-cauchy-theorem-one-exceptional-point-on-a-star-shaped-domain]]
+
+[Adams et al., *Causality, Analyticity and an IR Obstruction to UV Completion*, §4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0602178)
+
+[de Rham et al., *Positivity Bounds for Scalar Theories*, §§2.1–2.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1702.06134)
+
+[Basile et al., *Lectures in Quantum Gravity*, §§3.2.1–3.3.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.08690.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The amplitude data with $m>0$, gap $\nu_0=2m^2$, paired real subthreshold simple-pole subtraction, crossing, real analyticity, the exact $o(|v|^2)$ upper-half-plane condition, and the stated Riemann boundary and convergence hypotheses.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-scattering-amplitude-input]]: $B$ is holomorphic on the two-cut domain, real analytic, and has $\rho(\nu)=\operatorname{Im}B(\nu+i0)\ge0$ with $\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-3}\,d\nu<\infty$; all specified variable subthreshold poles are subtracted and the endpoint caps vanish.
+
+[F2] [[lem-aqft-crossing-even-forward-amplitude]]: $B(-v)=B(v)$ and $B'(0)=0$.
+
+[F3] [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]: if $f$ is holomorphic on a disc and $\gamma$ is a positively oriented compactly contained circle, then $f(z)=(2\pi i)^{-1}\int_\gamma f(\zeta)/(\zeta-z)\,d\zeta$ for interior $z$.
+
+[F4] [[lem-cauchy-difference-quotient-exceptional-extension]]: for holomorphic $f$ near $z_0$, the quotient $(f(z)-f(z_0))/(z-z_0)$, filled by $f'(z_0)$, is continuous on the domain and holomorphic away from $z_0$.
+
+[F5] [[thm-cauchy-theorem-one-exceptional-point-on-a-star-shaped-domain]]: a continuous function holomorphic away from one point in an open star-shaped domain has zero integral around every closed rectifiable contour in that domain.
+
+[F6] [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]: $U$ is star-shaped with respect to $a\in U$ when $a+t(x-a)\in U$ for every $x\in U$ and $0\le t\le1$; an open half-plane has this property.
+
+[F7] [[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]: a right-unbounded Riemann integral is the finite limit of its truncation integrals.
+
+[F8] [[thm-linearity-of-improper-integrals]]: convergent improper Riemann integrals on the same one-ended interval are linear under finite real combinations.
+
+[F9] [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]: on a finite interval, a nonnegative Riemann-integrable function has nonnegative integral.
+
+[F10] [[thm-comparison-test-for-improper-integrals]]: if $0\le f\le g$ eventually and the improper integral of $g$ converges, then that of $f$ converges.
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 Apply [F3] to $B$ on any circle centered at $0$ with radius $r<\nu_0$. Expanding $(\zeta-v)^{-1}=\sum_{j\ge0}v^j\zeta^{-j-1}$ uniformly on that circle for $|v|<r$ gives the Taylor series of $B$ near $0$. By [F2], every odd coefficient vanishes. Hence $H(v):=(B(v)-B(0))/v^2$ has a holomorphic extension through $v=0$ with $H(0)=B''(0)/2!$, and $H$ is real on the real gap. [F2, F3, given, algebra]
+
+2.1 Fix a real $v\in(-\nu_0,\nu_0)$, $R>\nu_0+|v|$, $0<\eta<\min(1,\nu_0-|v|)$, and $\delta>0$. Let $U_\delta=\{z\in\mathbb C:\operatorname{Im}z>-\delta/2\}$, an open half-plane and thus star-shaped by [F6]. The closed contour $\Gamma_{R,\eta}$ consists of the real segments from $v-R$ to $v-\eta$ and $v+\eta$ to $v+R$, the upper semicircle of radius $\eta$ about $v$ traversed left-to-right, and the upper semicircle of radius $R$ about $v$ traversed right-to-left. It lies in $U_\delta$. The function $G_\delta(z)=(H(z+i\delta)-H(v+i\delta))/(z-v)$, filled at $z=v$, is continuous on $U_\delta$ and holomorphic away from $v$ by [F4], because $z+i\delta$ lies in the upper half-plane. [F4, F5, F6, given, construct]
+
+2.2 Apply [F5] to $G_\delta$ on $\Gamma_{R,\eta}$. The two real segments contribute $\log(\eta/R)+\log(R/\eta)=0$ to $\int dz/(z-v)$; the small upper semicircle contributes $-i\pi$ and the large upper semicircle contributes $+i\pi$. Thus $\int_{\Gamma_{R,\eta}}dz/(z-v)=0$, and the definition of $G_\delta$ gives $\int_{\Gamma_{R,\eta}}H(z+i\delta)/(z-v)\,dz=0$. [step 2.1, F5, algebra]
+
+3.1 Let $\delta\downarrow0$, then let $R\to\infty$ and $\eta\downarrow0$. The cut-boundary hypotheses in [F1] give the upper boundary values on the real segments; the small semicircle tends to $-i\pi H(v)$ because $H$ is holomorphic at $v$; and the large semicircle tends to zero since $H(w)=o(1)$ and its kernel denominator has modulus $R$. Therefore $\operatorname{PV}\!\int_{\mathbb R}H_+(x)/(x-v)\,dx=i\pi H(v)$, where $H_+(x)$ denotes the upper boundary value. Taking imaginary parts and using $H(v)\in\mathbb R$ yields $H(v)=\pi^{-1}\operatorname{PV}\int_{\mathbb R}\operatorname{Im}H_+(x)/(x-v)\,dx$. [step 2.2, F1, given, algebra]
+
+3.2 On the gap, $\operatorname{Im}H_+(x)=0$. On the right cut, $\operatorname{Im}H_+(\nu)=\rho(\nu)/\nu^2$. On the left cut, evenness and real analyticity give $\operatorname{Im}H_+(-\nu)=-\rho(\nu)/\nu^2$. Substituting these boundary values into step 3.1 and changing variables $x=-\nu$ on the left cut gives $H(v)=\pi^{-1}\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-2}\bigl((\nu-v)^{-1}+(\nu+v)^{-1}\bigr)\,d\nu=(2/\pi)\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)/[\nu(\nu^2-v^2)]\,d\nu$. Both integrals converge: for fixed $|v|<\nu_0$ their kernels are bounded near threshold, and at infinity they are bounded by a constant times $\nu^{-3}$; [F7, F10] apply. This is the twice-subtracted forward dispersion relation, with subtraction constants $B(0)$ and $B'(0)=0$ and with all specified subthreshold poles removed. [step 3.1, F1, F2, F7, F10, algebra]
+
+4.1 For real $|v|<\nu_0$, expand $1/(1-v^2/\nu^2)$ through order $N$; its remainder is bounded uniformly in $\nu\ge\nu_0$ by $q^{N+1}/(1-q)$, where $q=v^2/\nu_0^2<1$. Using [F8] for the finite sum and [F9, F10] for the nonnegative remainder gives $B(v)=B(0)+\sum_{n=1}^{N+1}\bigl((2/\pi)\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-2n-1}\,d\nu\bigr)v^{2n}+o(v^{2N+2})$ as $v\to0$ through real values. The Taylor series of $B$ from step 1.1 is unique: if a first coefficient differed, dividing the difference by its least differing power and taking $v\to0$ would give a nonzero constant equal to $0$. Therefore $B^{(2n)}(0)/(2n)!=(2/\pi)\int_{\nu_0}^{\infty}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-2n-1}d\nu$ for every $n\ge1$. [step 1.1, step 3.2, F8, F9, F10, algebra]
+
+5.1 For each finite $R>\nu_0$, the integrand $\rho(\nu)/\nu^{2n+1}$ is nonnegative, so [F9] gives $\int_{\nu_0}^{R}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-2n-1}d\nu\ge0$. Its improper limit is finite by [F10], since $0\le\rho(\nu)\nu^{-2n-1}\le\nu_0^{-2n+2}\rho(\nu)\nu^{-3}$. Taking that limit and multiplying by $2/\pi>0$ proves the asserted nonnegativity. If $\rho$ vanishes, equality can occur; strict positivity is not concluded. [F1, F9, F10, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+The contour identity separates the two physical cuts only after the crossing-even pole subtraction and the explicit two-subtraction high-energy condition. A massless pole or cut at $t=0$, a nonvanishing outer contribution, or a nonpositive absorptive input invalidates this proof route. The result constrains forward on-shell amplitude combinations; it does not assign a universal sign to all Wilson coefficients.
+
+The diagram shows the real $z$-plane contour $\Gamma_{R,\eta}$ used in step 2.1, with $R>|v|+\nu_0$ and $0<\eta<\nu_0-|v|$. The contour's straight segments cross the displayed real cuts, but the integrand there is $H(z+i\delta)$, so its amplitude argument is shifted into the upper half-plane. The small upper semicircle is traversed left-to-right and the large one right-to-left. The proof uses the stated contour integrals and orientations, not the drawing.
+
+```tikz
+\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.65cm,>=stealth]
+  \draw[->] (-4.4,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$\operatorname{Re}z$};
