      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The amplitude data with $m>0$, gap $\\nu_0=2m^2$, paired real subthreshold simple-pole subtraction, crossing, real analyticity, the exact $o(|v|^2)$ upper-half-plane condition, and the stated Riemann boundary and convergence hypotheses.\n\n[F1] [[def-aqft-scattering-amplitude-input]]: $B$ is holomorphic on the two-cut domain, real analytic, and has $\\rho(\\nu)=\\operatorname{Im}B(\\nu+i0)\\ge0$ with $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}\\,d\\nu<\\infty$; all specified variable subthreshold poles are subtracted and the endpoint caps vanish.\n\n[F2] [[lem-aqft-crossing-even-forward-amplitude]]: $B(-v)=B(v)$ and $B'(0)=0$.\n\n[F3] [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]]: if $f$ is holomorphic on a disc and $\\gamma$ is a positively oriented compactly contained circle, then $f(z)=(2\\pi i)^{-1}\\int_\\gamma f(\\zeta)/(\\zeta-z)\\,d\\zeta$ for interior $z$.\n\n[F4] [[lem-cauchy-difference-quotient-exceptional-extension]]: for holomorphic $f$ near $z_0$, the quotient $(f(z)-f(z_0))/(z-z_0)$, filled by $f'(z_0)$, is continuous on the domain and holomorphic away from $z_0$.\n\n[F5] [[thm-cauchy-theorem-one-exceptional-point-on-a-star-shaped-domain]]: a continuous function holomorphic away from one point in an open star-shaped domain has zero integral around every closed rectifiable contour in that domain.\n\n[F6] [[def-star-shaped-open-subset-of-rn]]: $U$ is star-shaped with respect to $a\\in U$ when $a+t(x-a)\\in U$ for every $x\\in U$ and $0\\le t\\le1$; an open half-plane has this property.\n\n[F7] [[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]: a right-unbounded Riemann integral is the finite limit of its truncation integrals.\n\n[F8] [[thm-linearity-of-improper-integrals]]: convergent improper Riemann integrals on the same one-ended interval are linear under finite real combinations.\n\n[F9] [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]: on a finite interval, a nonnegative Riemann-integrable function has nonnegative integral.\n\n[F10] [[thm-comparison-test-for-improper-integrals]]: if $0\\le f\\le g$ eventually and the improper integral of $g$ converges, then that of $f$ converges.\n\n\n\n1.1 Apply [F3] to $B$ on any circle centered at $0$ with radius $r<\\nu_0$. Expanding $(\\zeta-v)^{-1}=\\sum_{j\\ge0}v^j\\zeta^{-j-1}$ uniformly on that circle for $|v|<r$ gives the Taylor series of $B$ near $0$. By [F2], every odd coefficient vanishes. Hence $H(v):=(B(v)-B(0))/v^2$ has a holomorphic extension through $v=0$ with $H(0)=B''(0)/2!$, and $H$ is real on the real gap. [F2, F3, given, algebra]\n\n1.2 Fix a real $v\\in(-\\nu_0,\\nu_0)$, $R>\\nu_0+|v|$, $0<\\eta<\\min(1,\\nu_0-|v|)$, and $\\delta>0$. Let $U_\\delta=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:\\operatorname{Im}z>-\\delta/2\\}$, an open half-plane and thus star-shaped by [F6]. The closed contour $\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}$ consists of the real segments from $v-R$ to $v-\\eta$ and $v+\\eta$ to $v+R$, the upper semicircle of radius $\\eta$ about $v$ traversed left-to-right, and the upper semicircle of radius $R$ about $v$ traversed right-to-left. It lies in $U_\\delta$. The function $G_\\delta(z)=(H(z+i\\delta)-H(v+i\\delta))/(z-v)$, filled at $z=v$, is continuous on $U_\\delta$ and holomorphic away from $v$ by [F4], because $z+i\\delta$ lies in the upper half-plane. [F4, F5, F6, given, construct]\n\n2.1 Apply [F5] to $G_\\delta$ on $\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}$. The two real segments contribute $\\log(\\eta/R)+\\log(R/\\eta)=0$ to $\\int dz/(z-v)$; the small upper semicircle contributes $-i\\pi$ and the large upper semicircle contributes $+i\\pi$. Thus $\\int_{\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}}dz/(z-v)=0$, and the definition of $G_\\delta$ gives $\\int_{\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}}H(z+i\\delta)/(z-v)\\,dz=0$. [step 1.2, F5, algebra]\n\n3.1 Let $\\delta\\downarrow0$, then let $R\\to\\infty$ and $\\eta\\downarrow0$. The cut-boundary hypotheses in [F1] give the upper boundary values on the real segments; the small semicircle tends to $-i\\pi H(v)$ because $H$ is holomorphic at $v$; and the large semicircle tends to zero since $H(w)=o(1)$ and its kernel denominator has modulus $R$. Therefore $\\operatorname{PV}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R}H_+(x)/(x-v)\\,dx=i\\pi H(v)$, where $H_+(x)$ denotes the upper boundary value. Taking imaginary parts and using $H(v)\\in\\mathbb R$ yields $H(v)=\\pi^{-1}\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\operatorname{Im}H_+(x)/(x-v)\\,dx$. [step 2.1, F1, given, algebra]\n\n4.1 On the gap, $\\operatorname{Im}H_+(x)=0$. On the right cut, $\\operatorname{Im}H_+(\\nu)=\\rho(\\nu)/\\nu^2$. On the left cut, evenness and real analyticity give $\\operatorname{Im}H_+(-\\nu)=-\\rho(\\nu)/\\nu^2$. Substituting these boundary values into step 3.1 and changing variables $x=-\\nu$ on the left cut gives $H(v)=\\pi^{-1}\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2}\\bigl((\\nu-v)^{-1}+(\\nu+v)^{-1}\\bigr)\\,d\\nu=(2/\\pi)\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)/[\\nu(\\nu^2-v^2)]\\,d\\nu$. Both integrals converge: for fixed $|v|<\\nu_0$ their kernels are bounded near threshold, and at infinity they are bounded by a constant times $\\nu^{-3}$; [F7, F10] apply. This is the twice-subtracted forward dispersion relation, with subtraction constants $B(0)$ and $B'(0)=0$ and with all specified subthreshold poles removed. [step 3.1, F1, F2, F7, F10, algebra]\n\n5.1 For real $|v|<\\nu_0$, expand $1/(1-v^2/\\nu^2)$ through order $N$; its remainder is bounded uniformly in $\\nu\\ge\\nu_0$ by $q^{N+1}/(1-q)$, where $q=v^2/\\nu_0^2<1$. Using [F8] for the finite sum and [F9, F10] for the nonnegative remainder gives $B(v)=B(0)+\\sum_{n=1}^{N+1}\\bigl((2/\\pi)\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2n-1}\\,d\\nu\\bigr)v^{2n}+o(v^{2N+2})$ as $v\\to0$ through real values. The Taylor series of $B$ from step 1.1 is unique: if a first coefficient differed, dividing the difference by its least differing power and taking $v\\to0$ would give a nonzero constant equal to $0$. Therefore $B^{(2n)}(0)/(2n)!=(2/\\pi)\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2n-1}d\\nu$ for every $n\\ge1$. [step 1.1, step 4.1, F8, F9, F10, algebra]\n\n6.1 For each finite $R>\\nu_0$, the integrand $\\rho(\\nu)/\\nu^{2n+1}$ is nonnegative, so [F9] gives $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{R}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2n-1}d\\nu\\ge0$. Its improper limit is finite by [F10], since $0\\le\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2n-1}\\le\\nu_0^{-2n+2}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}$. Taking that limit and multiplying by $2/\\pi>0$ proves the asserted nonnegativity. If $\\rho$ vanishes, equality can occur; strict positivity is not concluded. [F1, F9, F10, step 5.1, algebra] ∎\n\n\n\nThe contour identity separates the two physical cuts only after the crossing-even pole subtraction and the explicit two-subtraction high-energy condition. A massless pole or cut at $t=0$, a nonvanishing outer contribution, or a nonpositive absorptive input invalidates this proof route. The result constrains forward on-shell amplitude combinations; it does not assign a universal sign to all Wilson coefficients.\n\nThe diagram shows the real $z$-plane contour $\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}$ used in step 1.2, with $R>|v|+\\nu_0$ and $0<\\eta<\\nu_0-|v|$. The contour's straight segments cross the displayed real cuts, but the integrand there is $H(z+i\\delta)$, so its amplitude argument is shifted into the upper half-plane. The small upper semicircle is traversed left-to-right and the large one right-to-left. The proof uses the stated contour integrals and orientations, not the drawing.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.65cm,>=stealth]\n  \\draw[->] (-4.4,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$\\operatorname{Re}z$};\n  \\draw[very thick] (-4.0,0) -- (-2.0,0);\n  \\draw[very thick] (2.0,0) -- (4.0,0);\n  \\node[below] at (-2.0,0) {$-\\nu_0$};\n  \\node[below] at (2.0,0) {$\\nu_0$};\n  \\node[below] at (0.45,0) {$v$};\n  \\draw[thick] (-2.85,0) -- (-0.10,0);\n  \\draw[->,thick] (-1.65,0) -- (-1.20,0);\n  \\draw[thick] (1.00,0) -- (3.75,0);\n  \\draw[->,thick] (2.20,0) -- (2.65,0);\n  \\draw[thick] (-0.10,0) arc (180:0:0.55);\n  \\draw[->,thick] (0.30,0.36) -- (0.60,0.36);\n  \\draw[thick] (3.75,0) arc (0:180:3.30);\n  \\draw[->,thick] (0.75,2.15) -- (0.35,2.15);\n  \\node[above] at (0.45,0.55) {$\\eta$};\n  \\node[above] at (0.45,3.35) {$R$};\n  \\node[above right] at (3.2,2.2) {$\\Gamma_{R,\\eta}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```"
