      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The amplitude data of the preceding definition, integer $N\\ge1$, and arbitrary real vectors $a=(a_0,\\ldots,a_r)$ in the indicated ranges.\n\n[F1] [[def-aqft-scattering-amplitude-input]] and [[def-aqft-truncated-amplitude-bootstrap-problem]]: $\\nu\\ge\\nu_0>0$, $\\rho(\\nu)\\ge0$, $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-3}\\,d\\nu<\\infty$, and $b_k=\\nu_0^{2k+2}c_{k+1}$; $\\mathcal K_N$ consists exactly of the three stated families of inequalities with the given index bounds.\n\n[F2] [[thm-aqft-gapped-scalar-forward-positivity]]: under these data, for every integer $n\\ge1$, $c_n=(2/\\pi)\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2n-1}\\,d\\nu$, with a finite improper Riemann integral.\n\n[F3] [[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]: the integral on $[\\nu_0,\\infty)$ is the finite limit of its truncations on $[\\nu_0,R]$ as $R\\to\\infty$.\n\n[F4] [[thm-linearity-of-improper-integrals]]: finite linear combinations of convergent improper Riemann integrals on this interval equal the integral of the corresponding linear combination.\n\n[F5] [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]]: the integral of a nonnegative Riemann-integrable function on a finite interval is nonnegative.\n\n[F6] [[thm-comparison-test-for-improper-integrals]]: if $0\\le f\\le g$ eventually and the improper integral of $g$ converges, then the improper integral of $f$ converges.\n\n\n\n1.1 For $\\nu\\ge\\nu_0$, put $x(\\nu)=\\nu_0^2/\\nu^2$ and $w(\\nu)=(2\\nu_0^2/\\pi)\\rho(\\nu)/\\nu^3$. Then $0<x(\\nu)\\le1$, $w(\\nu)\\ge0$, and $\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}w(\\nu)\\,d\\nu<\\infty$ by [F1] and [F3]. For every $0\\le k\\le N$, [F2] gives $b_k=\\nu_0^{2k+2}c_{k+1}=(2\\nu_0^{2k+2}/\\pi)\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}\\rho(\\nu)\\nu^{-2k-3}\\,d\\nu=\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}w(\\nu)x(\\nu)^k\\,d\\nu$. These moments are finite since $0\\le x^k\\le1$. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Fix $r\\ge0$ with $2r\\le N$ and real $a_0,\\ldots,a_r$. Let $p(x)=\\sum_{i=0}^r a_i x^i$. Using the finitely many moment identities from step 1.1 and [F4], $\\sum_{i,j=0}^r a_i a_j b_{i+j}=\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}w(\\nu)p(x(\\nu))^2\\,d\\nu$. The polynomial $p$ is bounded on $[0,1]$, so the integrand is bounded above by a fixed multiple of the integrable nonnegative function $w$; [F6] gives convergence. Its finite truncations are nonnegative by [F5], and their limit is nonnegative by [F3]. Thus the first family holds. [F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Fix $r\\ge0$ with $2r+1\\le N$ and the same arbitrary vector, and use $p(x)=\\sum_{i=0}^r a_i x^i$. The moment identities and [F4] give $\\sum_{i,j=0}^r a_i a_j b_{i+j+1}=\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}w(\\nu)x(\\nu)p(x(\\nu))^2\\,d\\nu$ and $\\sum_{i,j=0}^r a_i a_j(b_{i+j}-b_{i+j+1})=\\int_{\\nu_0}^{\\infty}w(\\nu)(1-x(\\nu))p(x(\\nu))^2\\,d\\nu$. The index bound ensures every moment used has index at most $N$. Since $0<x(\\nu)\\le1$, both integrands are nonnegative. Each is bounded by $Cw(\\nu)$ for $C=\\sup_{x\\in[0,1]}p(x)^2<\\infty$, so [F6] and [F3] give convergent nonnegative improper integrals, using [F5] on every finite truncation. Both remaining families therefore hold. At threshold $x=1$ the second localizer vanishes, and as $\\nu\\to\\infty$ one has $x\\to0$; neither endpoint changes the nonnegative-integral argument. [F3, F4, F5, F6, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For $N=1$, all three applicable families have $r=0$ and are included in steps 2.1 and 2.2. For every $N\\ge1$, those steps establish every inequality in the defining index ranges, so the vector belongs to $\\mathcal K_N$. Equality may occur when an integrand vanishes; the proof gives no strict inequality. The set $\\mathcal K_N$ was defined only by these necessary tests, so this membership proves no converse or full-amplitude realization. [F1, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra] ∎"
